M2-c010 L01 M1 | card: Must a sequence with limit L reach L at some finite step? | item: “A sequence with limit \(L\) must reach \(L\) at some finite step.”
M2-c011 L01 M2 | card: Do the first terms of a convergent sequence help determine its limit? | item: “The first terms of a sequence help determine its limit.”
M2-c012 L01 M3 | card: Is a bounded sequence convergent? | item: “A bounded sequence must converge.”
M2-c018 L02 M1 | card: Is the limit of a quotient the quotient of the limits, whatever the de | item: “The limit of a quotient is the quotient of the limits, whatever the denominator does.”
M2-c019 L02 M2 | card: Does a sum of two divergent sequences always diverge? | item: “A sum converges only when each of its terms converges.”
M2-c024 L02 M3 | card: Does a limit at a point make a function continuous there? | item: “A function is continuous at \(a\) whenever its formula has a limit at \(a\).”
M2-c037 L03 M1 | card: Does the limit at a point depend on the value f(a)? | item: “The limit at \(a\) depends on the value \(f(a)\).”
M2-c038 L03 M2 | card: Does a bounded function have a limit at every point? | item: “A bounded function must have a limit at every point.”
M2-c039 L03 M3 | card: Does the right-hand limit at a equal f(a) whenever f is defined at a? | item: “The right-hand limit equals \(f(a)\) whenever \(f\) is defined at \(a\).”
M2-c048 L04 M1 | card: Does every function given by a formula have a derivative at every poin | item: “Every function given by a formula has a derivative at every point.”
M2-c049 L04 M2 | card: Is every continuous function differentiable? | item: “A continuous function is differentiable.”
M2-c050 L04 M3 | card: Is the quotient at h = 0.001 for x^3 at a = 1 the derivative? | item: “The quotient at \(h = 0.001\) is the derivative.”
M2-c059 L05 M1 | card: Is the derivative of a product the product of the derivatives? | item: “The derivative of a product is the product of the derivatives.”
M2-c060 L05 M2 | card: Does the chain rule differentiate the outer function at a? | item: “The chain rule differentiates the outer function at the point \(a\).”
M2-c061 L05 M3 | card: Is the central difference exact for x^3? | item: “A central difference gives the derivative exactly for a cubic.”
M2-c068 L06 M2 | card: Is a positive derivative necessary for a function to be strictly incre | item: “A strictly increasing function must have a positive derivative everywhere.”
M2-c071 L06 M1 | card: Does a zero derivative at a point produce a local extremum there? | item: “A zero derivative at a point produces a local extremum there.”
M2-c072 L06 M3 | card: Is the point c of the mean value theorem always the midpoint of [a, b] | item: “The point \(c\) of the mean value theorem is the midpoint of \([a, b]\).”
M2-c094 L07 M1 | card: Why can f' exist at a point where f'' does not? | item: “If \(f'\) exists everywhere, then \(f''\) exists everywhere.”
M2-c095 L07 M2 | card: Does the degree-n Taylor polynomial at 0 depend only on f(0)? | item: “The Taylor polynomial at 0 depends only on \(f(0)\).”
M2-c096 L07 M3 | card: Does a higher-degree Taylor polynomial approximate better at every poi | item: “A higher-degree Taylor polynomial is a better approximation at every point.”
